  <!-- COMMON MASONRY PROBLEMS -->
  <section class="lp-section lp-problems" style="background:#F5F3EF;border-top:1px solid #D8D2C8;">
    <div class="lp-section-inner">
      <div class="lp-section-label">Common Masonry Problems in Chicago</div>
      <h2 class="lp-section-h2">What We Fix — <em>Most Often in Chicago</em></h2>
      <div class="lp-problems-grid">
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Chicago Common Brick Spalling from Portland Cement Over-Application</h3>
          <p>Portland cement applied over soft Chicago Common brick traps moisture at the joint interface and forces the brick face to delaminate — irreversible damage. Correcting it means removing incompatible mortar and repointing with lime-based material.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Greystone Limestone Joint Failure and Water Infiltration</h3>
          <p>Greystone limestone cracks and spalls when repaired with Portland cement — it requires soft lime putty mortar, not standard tuckpointing compounds. Joint failure allows water behind the stone and causes interior damage.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Two-Flat Parapet and Coping Deterioration</h3>
          <p>Two-flat parapets face full sun, rain, and 90+ freeze-thaw cycles year-round — the most exposed masonry on the building. Coping and parapet joint failure lets water enter at the roofline, causing interior damage far more costly than early tuckpointing.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Terracotta Ornamental Joint Cracking</h3>
          <p>Decorative terracotta cornices and ornamental accents require specialist mortar formulations — standard brick or greystone compounds are not appropriate. Failed terracotta joints allow water infiltration and can destabilize the element structurally.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

